WEBVTT

00:04.650 --> 00:07.662
This is Allen Company first-aid training on burns.

00:07.662 --> 00:10.588
At a quarry, burns come from hot engine parts,

00:10.588 --> 00:13.725
welding and cutting, fires, hot fluids,

00:13.725 --> 00:16.837
battery acid and other chemicals, and electricity.

00:17.977 --> 00:19.914
This is first-aid awareness training.

00:19.914 --> 00:23.401
It follows first-aid methods acceptable to MSHA,

00:23.401 --> 00:25.952
based on the American Heart Association first-aid

00:25.952 --> 00:28.814
guidelines that Allen uses for hands-on training.

00:28.814 --> 00:31.776
It supports, but does not replace, your hands-on

00:31.776 --> 00:34.139
first-aid practice with the safety manager,

00:34.139 --> 00:36.114
the same day as this course.

00:37.242 --> 00:40.230
Burns are judged by depth. A superficial burn is

00:40.230 --> 00:43.630
red and painful, like sunburn. A partial-thickness

00:43.630 --> 00:46.443
burn blisters. A full-thickness burn goes through

00:46.443 --> 00:49.805
the skin and may look white, brown or charred,

00:49.805 --> 00:52.393
and may not hurt because nerves are damaged.

00:52.393 --> 00:54.968
Any large burn, any burn to the face,

00:54.968 --> 00:58.992
hands, feet, groin or a joint, and any full-thickness

00:58.992 --> 01:00.655
burn needs medical care.

01:03.989 --> 01:06.376
Stop the burning: get the person away from the

01:06.376 --> 01:09.226
heat; if clothes are on fire, stop,

01:09.226 --> 01:11.976
drop and roll, or smother the flames.

01:11.976 --> 01:14.588
Cool the burn with cool or cold clean water as

01:14.588 --> 01:17.439
soon as possible, for at least 10 minutes.

01:17.439 --> 01:20.314
Never put ice on a burn. Remove rings and tight

01:20.314 --> 01:23.213
items before swelling starts, but don't pull off

01:23.213 --> 01:25.838
clothing stuck to the skin. Cover loosely with

01:25.838 --> 01:29.576
a clean, dry dressing. No ice, no butter,

01:29.576 --> 01:32.126
no ointments, and don't break blisters.

01:35.500 --> 01:38.413
For chemical burns, brush off any dry chemical

01:38.413 --> 01:41.225
first, then flush right away with large amounts

01:41.225 --> 01:44.488
of water, removing contaminated clothing and jewelry

01:44.488 --> 01:46.775
while you flush, and keep flushing.

01:46.775 --> 01:49.475
Check the safety data sheet, section 4,

01:49.475 --> 01:52.538
and send the SDS with the person to the hospital.

01:55.873 --> 01:58.422
With an electrical injury, don't touch the person

01:58.422 --> 02:01.248
until the power is off. Electrical burns can look

02:01.248 --> 02:04.398
small on the outside, with an entry and an exit

02:04.398 --> 02:07.823
wound, but cause deep damage and stop the heart.

02:07.823 --> 02:12.173
Call 911, be ready for CPR, and get them to a hospital

02:12.173 --> 02:13.648
even if they seem fine.

02:16.966 --> 02:20.803
Recap: stop, cool, cover. Ten minutes or more of

02:20.803 --> 02:24.128
cool water for heat burns, longer for chemicals.

02:24.128 --> 02:27.528
Nothing greasy, no ice, don't pop blisters.

02:27.528 --> 02:30.278
Power off before you touch an electrical victim.

02:30.278 --> 02:32.328
Get serious burns to a doctor.

02:33.448 --> 02:36.610
Remember: your site's plans and procedures always

02:36.610 --> 02:38.111
override this video.
